    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I get the systemic stuff, but if we're looking at the statistical reality of the gaps, some researchers argue that we can't just blame systemic factors alone. They suggest looking at whether there are any underlying differences in cognitive development or even genetic predispositions that might cause these patterns to persist regardless of socioeconomic fixes. From a purely data-driven standpoint, how do people reconcile those more \"biological\" arguments with the sociological ones you're mentioning?",
